#  Text

BEGIN_PGML


You want to maximize [` f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 + 4y `] on the closed disk [` A = \{ (x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 : x^2+y^2 \leq 16\} `]. Semeon has written out an argument. All of his derivatives and algebraic manipulations are correct.

---

1. _Since [` f `] is [` C^1 `] and [` \nabla f(x,y) = (2x, 2y+4) `], the critical points of [` f `] occur when [` (2x,2y+4)=(0,0) `]._
2. _Hence, [` (x,y) = (0,-2) `] is the only critical point of [` f `] on [` A `]._
3. _The boundary of [` A `] is the circle [` \partial A = \{ (x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 : g(x,y) = 16 \} `] where [` g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 `]._
4. _The system of equations [` \nabla f(x,y) = \lambda \nabla g(x,y), \hspace{2mm} g(x,y) = 16 `] with [` x,y,\lambda \in \mathbb{R} `] is equivalent to [` 2x = 2 \lambda x, \hspace{2mm} 2y+4 = 2\lambda y, \hspace{2mm} x^2+y^2=16. `]_
5. _The first equation implies [` 2x(\lambda-1) = 0 `] so [` \lambda = 1 `] or [` x=0 `]._
6. _If [` \lambda = 1 `], then the second equation implies [` 4=0 `] so there are no solutions in this case._
7. _If [` x=0 `], then the third equation implies [` y^2=16 `] so [` y = \pm 4 `]._
8. _Thus, the only possible solutions to the system are [` (x,y) = (0,\pm 4) `] with corresponding [` \lambda `] values._
1. _Thus, the maximum of [` f `] on [` \partial A `] must occur at one of [` (0,4) `] or [` (0,-4) `]._
1. _Overall, the maximum of [` f `] on [` A `] must occur at one of [` (0,-2), (0,4) `] or [` (0,-4) `]._
1. _By direct computation, [` f(0,-2) = -4, f(0,4) = 32 `] and [` f(0,-4) = 0 `]._
1. _The maximum of [` f `] on [` A `] is therefore 32._

---

Semeon’s argument does not have a serious logical error, but it is missing important justifications.

Identify which line(s) require the global extreme value theorem. For multiple lines, separate the numbers in ascending order with commas and without spaces.

[________]{'9,10,12'}

